/*! \page developer_backend_math_page Vector and Reduction Operations

\ref developer_backend_field_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_comm_page  (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

PSCF provides a set of templates for classes and functions that perform 
basic mathematical operations on arrays of numbers. The FFT class template,
which performs discrete Fourier transforms, has been discussed on the
previous page.  This page discusses functions that perform two additional
types of operation that we refer to as vector operations and reductions:

   - <b> Vector operations </b> take one or more arrays as inputs and 
     produce a single array as an output, such that each element of the 
     output array can be computed elementwise from corresponding 
     elements of all input arrays. Such operations include vector-vector 
     addition, vector-scalar multiplication, and elementwise vector-vector 
     multiplication. Vector operations are performed by functions defined
     in namespace Pscf::VecOp.

   - <b> Reduction operations </b>: take one or more arrays as inputs 
     and return a scalar output. Such operations include calculation 
     of a dot product of two arrays and identification of minimum or 
     maximum elements of an array. Reduction operations are performed
     by functions defined namespace Pscf::Reduce.

For each function name that is defined in the Pscf::VecOp and Pscf::Reduce
namespaces, overloaded versions are defined that are designed to be used 
with different backends. Versions of each functions that are designed to 
be used with different backends are distinguished by the types declared 
for their array-valued input and output parameters:

   - Functions that are designed to be used with a CUDA backend 
     take array parameters with types that are specializations of 
     Pscf::DeviceArray \<Data, Pscf::CUT \>, with Data = cudaReal for 
     arrays with real elements and Data = cudaComplex for arrays with 
     complex elements.

   - Functions that are designed to be used with a CUDA backend instead
     take array parameters with types that are specializations of 
     Util::Array \<Data \>, with Data = double for arrays with real 
     elements and Data = fftw_complex for arrays with complex elements.

These interfaces allow RField, RFieldDft and CField classes to be passed
to such functions because the field classes with a backend identifier
class T=Pscf::CUT that are used with a Cuda backend are all derived 
from specializations of DeviceArray \<Data, T\> with T=Pscf::CUT, while 
the field classes with T=PSCF::CPT are all indirectly derived from 
corresponding specializations of Util::Array \<Data\>. 

Vector operation and reduction functions that are designed for use with a 
CUDA backend are defined in files located in the src/pscf/backend/cuda 
directory. Corresponding functions that are designed for use with a C++
backend are defined in files located in the src/pscf/backend/cpp directory.

<b> Example </b>: 

The function Pscf::VecOp::addVV is used to perform
binary vector-vector addition.  The file src/pscf/backend/cpp/VecOp.h 
declares a version of this function that uses a C++ backend to add two 
real arrays, which is declared within namespace Pscf::VecOp as:
\code
    void addVV(Array<double>& a, 
               Array<double> const & b, 
               Array<double> const & c);
\endcode
where Array<double> is used to denote Util::Array<double>.
The file src/pscf/backend/cuda/VecOp.h declares an overloaded version 
of this function that uses a Cuda backend, which is declared in 
namespace Pscf::VecOp as
\code
    void addVV(DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT>& a, 
               DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT> const & b, 
               DeviceArray<cudaReal, CUT> const & c);
\endcode
where DeviceArray<cudaReal> is used to denotes Pscf::DeviceArray<cudaReal>.
Two other overloaded versions of the function addVV are also defined that
can perform addition of complex-valued arrays using either backend,
though the declarations for these overloads are not shown here. 
In the interface for addVV and almost all other functions in namespace 
VecOp, the first parameter (a) is an output parameter that is used to 
return the result of a calculation, which is passed to the function by 
non-const reference, while the remaining parameters are input 
parameters.

As shown in this example, analogous functions that perform the same 
mathematical operation but use different backends are assigned the same 
function name, thus defining overloaded variants of that function. This 
usage relies on the C++ system for function overload resolution to find 
the correct version of each function by inspecting the types of the 
arguments passed to the function in code where the function is called. 
For example, suppose that the function Pscf::VecOp::addVV is used to add 
two real-valued arrays (or vectors) within a member function of a class 
template that takes a backend identifier class T as a template parameter. 
In specializations of that class template with T=Pscf::CPT, the addVV 
will always be passed arguments that are instances of subclasses of 
Util::Array<double>. In specializations with T=Pscf::CUT, the same
function invocation may be passed arguments that are instances of the 
class DeviceArray<cudaReal,CUT> or its subclasses.  The template type
system used by PSCF, in which different data types are used for arrays 
in specializations that use different backends, allows the compiler to 
choose the correct overload for functions defined in Pscf::VecOp and 
Pscf::Reduce when instantiating each such template specialization.

<b> Function Name Conventions </b>: 

The names of functions in namespace Pscf::VecOp follow a scheme in which
function names usually starts with a shortened form of the name of a 
mathematical operation (e.g., "add" for addition or "mul" for 
multiplication) followed by a code that indicates the nature of the 
inputs (e.g. "VV" for an operations with two vector inputs or "VS" for 
an operation with a vector and scalar input).  The function name "addVV" 
thus indicates a function that adds to vectors, while "mulVS" is a 
function that performs vector-scalar multiplication. The string "Eq" 
can be added after the name of the binary operation to indicate 
functions in which the first argument is used as an input that is
modified to store the output, as done by the C compound arithmetic 
operators such as +=, *=, etc.  The function addEqV(a,b) thus yields 
an ouput array a for which a[i] += b[i] for each element with index 
i. 

Conventions used for function names and argument types in the 
Pscf::VecOp and Pscf::Reduce namespaces are discussed in more detail 
in the doxygen documention for these namespaces.

\see Pscf_Cuda_VecOp_Module
\see Pscf_Cuda_Reduce_Module
\see Pscf_Cpp_VecOp_Module
\see Pscf_Cpp_Reduce_Module

<BR>
